Notebook[{Cell["FermiWick", "Title"], 
  Cell["A guided tour: from the ladder operators to a solved model", 
   "Subtitle"], Cell["0. Welcome", "Section"], Cell["FermiWick lets you write \
fermion operators as on paper and does the algebra for you. This tour starts \
from the ladder operators and builds everything up, one piece at a time: \
local operators and spins, a Hamiltonian, its exact solution, expectation \
values by Wick's theorem in the state you assume, mean field, and at the end \
a symbolic result checked against the exact one.", "Text"], 
  Cell["How to use this notebook: evaluate the cells in order (Shift+Enter, \
or Evaluation > Evaluate Notebook); the whole tour takes a few seconds. The \
comment under each input says what you should get. The same file runs as a \
script: math -script Examples.wl.", "Text"], Cell["Loading the package", 
   "Subsection"], Cell["(* the paclet next to this file (the repository), \
otherwise the installed one *)\nWith[{folder = Quiet[If[$InputFileName =!= \
\"\", DirectoryName[$InputFileName], NotebookDirectory[]]]},\n  \
If[StringQ[folder],\n   Scan[If[FileExistsQ[FileNameJoin[{#, \
\"PacletInfo.wl\"}]], PacletDirectoryLoad[#]] &, {FileNameJoin[{folder, \
\"FermiWick\"}], folder}]]];\nNeeds[\"FermiWick`\"]; \
Needs[\"FermiWick`Fock`\"]; Needs[\"FermiWick`Gutzwiller`\"];", "Input"], 
  Cell["$FermiWickVersion\n(* the version of the package, e.g. \"0.6.0\" *)", 
   "Input"], Cell["1. Ladder operators", "Section"], 
  Cell["c[i, s] annihilates a fermion on site i with spin s, and cDag[i, s] \
creates it; the spin is SpinUp, SpinDown or a symbol. ** multiplies in the \
order written, and NormalOrder brings every cDag to the left.", "Text"], 
  Cell["NormalOrder[c[1, SpinUp] ** cDag[1, SpinUp]]\n(* 1 - cDag[1, up] c[1, \
up]: the anticommutation relation *)", "Input"], 
  Cell["NormalOrder[cDag[1, SpinUp] ** cDag[1, SpinUp]]\n(* 0: the Pauli \
principle *)", "Input"], Cell["Sites can be symbols: different symbols are \
different sites, so a result holds on any lattice.", "Text"], 
  Cell[
   "NormalOrder[c[i, SpinUp] ** cDag[j, SpinUp]]\n(* -cDag[j, up] c[i, up] \
*)", "Input"], Cell["PlusHC[cDag[1, SpinUp] ** c[2, SpinUp]]\n(* the hopping \
from 2 to 1 plus its Hermitian conjugate *)", "Input"], 
  Cell["2. Local operators", "Section"], Cell["Products on one site are read \
as local operators, which stay compact: the densities nOp and nTotOp, the \
spin SzOp, SpOp, SmOp, the double occupancy DOp. ToSpinChargeBasis writes an \
expression with them.", "Text"], Cell["ToSpinChargeBasis[cDag[1, SpinUp] ** \
c[1, SpinUp] ** cDag[1, SpinDown] ** c[1, SpinDown]]\n(* DOp[1]: the double \
occupancy *)", "Input"], Cell["ToSpinChargeBasis[SdotS[1, 1]]\n(* 3/4 n - 3/2 \
D: a spin 1/2 lives only on a singly occupied site *)", "Input"], 
  Cell["3. Spins on several sites", "Section"], 
  Cell["Products on several sites are recognized as spin operators: the \
exchange, written with ladder operators, is S_i.S_j plus a density term.", 
   "Text"], Cell["ToSpinChargeBasis[1/2 Sum[cDag[i, s] ** c[i, r] ** cDag[j, \
r] ** c[j, s], {s, $Spins}, {r, $Spins}]]\n(* S_i.S_j + n_i n_j/4 *)", 
   "Input"], Cell["The algebra of spins comes for free: the commutator of two \
exchange terms that share a site is the scalar chirality.", "Text"], 
  Cell["ToSpinChargeBasis[OperatorCommutator[SdotS[1, 2], SdotS[2, 3]]]\n(* \
-I S1.(S2 x S3) *)", "Input"], Cell["4. A model, as on paper", "Section"], 
  Cell["The Hubbard model on a ring of L sites: hopping t between neighbours, \
plus its Hermitian conjugate, and the repulsion U on every site, written \
(U/2)(n - 1)^2, the form symmetric about half filling. The dimer, two sites \
joined once, is written apart.", "Text"], Cell["hubbard[L_] := -t \
Sum[PlusHC[cDag[i, s] ** c[Mod[i, L] + 1, s]], {i, L}, {s, $Spins}] + U/2 \
Sum[(nTotOp[i] - 1)^2, {i, L}];\ndimer = -t Sum[PlusHC[cDag[1, s] ** c[2, \
s]], {s, $Spins}] + U/2 Sum[(nTotOp[i] - 1)^2, {i, 2}];", "Input"], 
  Cell["HermitianOperatorQ[hubbard[4]]\n(* True *)", "Input"], 
  Cell["5. Solving it exactly", "Section"], Cell["FermiWick`Fock` writes the \
same expressions as sparse matrices: on the Fock space of the sites, or on a \
sector with a fixed number of particles N and spin Sz. The dimer with two \
electrons and Sz = 0, symbolically:", "Text"], 
  Cell["dimerSector = FockSector[FockSpace[{1, 2}], \"N\" -> 2, \"Sz\" -> \
0];\nEigenvalues[Normal[FockMatrix[dimer, dimerSector]]]\n(* 0, U and (U -+ \
Sqrt[U^2 + 16 t^2])/2 *)", "Input"], Cell["At a finite temperature everything \
follows from the grand-canonical partition function, the trace of Exp[-(H - \
mu N)/T] over the whole Fock space, sixteen states here: its logarithm is the \
free energy, at half filling for mu = 0, and derivatives give the entropy, \
the particle number, the compressibility.", "Text"], 
  Cell["F = FullSimplify[-T Log[PartitionFunction[dimer, FockSpace[{1, 2}], \
T]], T > 0]\n(* -T Log[2 Exp[-U/(2 T)] (4 Cosh[t/T] + 3 Cosh[U/(2 T)] + \
Cosh[Sqrt[16 t^2 + U^2]/(2 T)])] *)", "Input"], 
  Cell["Limit[-D[F, T], T -> Infinity]\n(* Log[16]: at a high temperature the \
sixteen states are equally likely *)", "Input"], 
  Cell["The single-particle Green's function, as a sum over the same levels \
(the Lehmann representation), in closed form at zero temperature: its four \
poles are the energies of adding and of removing an electron. With \
\"Temperature\" -> T the excited states contribute too.", "Text"], 
  Cell["FullSimplify[GreenMatrix[LehmannGreen[dimer, FockSpace[{1, 2}], {c[1, \
SpinUp]}], z][[1, 1]], t > 0 && U > 0]\n(* -4 z (36 t^2 + U^2 - 4 z^2)/((12 \
t^2 + U^2)^2 - 8 z^2 (20 t^2 + U^2) + 16 z^4) *)", "Input"], 
  Cell["A ring of ten sites at half filling, with U = 4t: a sector of 63 504 \
states, solved in about a second.", "Text"], 
  Cell["ringSector = FockSector[FockSpace[Range[10]], \"N\" -> 10, \"Sz\" -> \
0];\n{energies, vectors} = LowestStates[FockMatrix[hubbard[10] /. {t -> 1, U \
-> 4}, ringSector], 1];\nenergies\n(* {-5.834...}: the ground-state energy, \
in units of t *)", "Input"], Cell["ground = \
First[vectors];\n{FockExpectation[SdotS[1, 2], ground, ringSector], \
FockExpectation[DOp[1], ground, ringSector]}\n(* about {-0.32, 0.10}: \
antiferromagnetic neighbours, and few doubly occupied sites *)", "Input"], 
  Cell["6. Expectation values by Wick's theorem", "Section"], 
  Cell["Approximate states are Gaussian: fixed by their contractions, one \
amplitude each. GaussianState[] has them all, and ContractionTable shows them \
between the modes of some sites: on one site, the densities of each spin and \
<S^+> off the diagonal.", "Text"], Cell["MatrixForm[ContractionTable[Gaussian\
State[], {1}, \"Normal\"]]\n(* chi^{up up}_11, chi^{down down}_11 on the \
diagonal, chi^{up down}_11 = <S^+_1> and its conjugate off it *)", "Input"], 
  Cell["A symmetry you assume removes contractions, or the indices that told \
them apart: with the charge conserved there is no pairing; with S^z conserved \
the hopping keeps the spin and the pairing joins opposite spins; with spin \
rotations (SU(2)) there is one hopping chi[i, j] and the singlet pairing \
Delta[i, j]; with time reversal the amplitudes of opposite spins are \
conjugates, and with SU(2) real.", "Text"], Cell["MatrixForm[ContractionTable\
[GaussianState[\"SpinRotationZ\"], {1, 2}, \"Anomalous\"]]\n(* S^z conserved: \
the pairing joins opposite spins *)", "Input"], 
  Cell["The state of most calculations has a short name: SpinSingletState[] \
is GaussianState[{\"SpinRotation\", \"Charge\"}, \"Filling\" -> 1/2], \
invariant under spin rotations, the charge conserved, at half filling.", 
   "Text"], Cell["MatrixForm[ContractionTable[SpinSingletState[], {1, 2}, \
\"Normal\"]]\n(* 1/2 on each site, chi_12 between the sites, nothing between \
opposite spins *)", "Input"], Cell["WickVEV[dimer, SpinSingletState[]]\n(* -2 \
t (chi_12 + Conjugate[chi_12]) + U/2: the energy of the dimer in this state \
*)", "Input"], Cell["Products of any length: the correlation of two exchange \
terms, eight fermions, in a state with singlet pairing too, has sixty terms."\
, "Text"], Cell["big = WickVEV[SdotS[1, 2] ** SdotS[3, 4], \
GaussianState[\"SpinRotation\"]];\nLength[big]\n(* 60 *)", "Input"], 
  Cell["The symmetries decide what survives: the chirality needs a state that \
breaks time reversal.", "Text"], Cell["WickVEV[SdotScrossS[1, 2, 3], \
GaussianState[{\"SpinRotation\", \"Charge\"}]]\n(* -3 Im(chi_12 chi_23 \
chi_31), written with chi_ji = Conjugate[chi_ij] *)", "Input"], 
  Cell["WickVEV[SdotScrossS[1, 2, 3], GaussianState[{\"SpinRotation\", \
\"Charge\", \"TimeReversal\"}]]\n(* 0 *)", "Input"], 
  Cell["7. States by rules, and modes with any labels", "Section"], 
  Cell["A state can also be declared by rules for its contractions, with any \
spin structure: here a hopping g that keeps the spin and a hopping h that \
flips it.", "Text"], Cell["flip = GaussianState[{{cDag[i_, s_], c[j_, r_]} :> \
If[i === j, KroneckerDelta[s, r]/2, If[s === r, g, h]]}];\nWickVEV[SzOp[1] ** \
SzOp[2], flip]\n(* (h^2 - g^2)/2 *)", "Input"], 
  Cell["Modes can carry any labels; DeclareModeLabels says what each label \
is. With an orbital, the anticommutator gives a delta of the orbitals:", 
   "Text"], Cell["DeclareModeLabels[{\"Site\", \"Orbital\", \
\"Spin\"}];\nNormalOrder[c[1, a, SpinUp] ** cDag[1, b, SpinUp]]\n(* \
KroneckerDelta[a, b] - cDag[1, b, up] c[1, a, up] *)", "Input"], 
  Cell["8. Mean field", "Section"], Cell["MeanField gives the \
Hartree-Fock-Bogoliubov Hamiltonian of an operator of any order: two \
operators kept out in every way, the rest contracted, and the constant that \
keeps the energy right. The Hubbard interaction in a state with magnetization \
m along z:", "Text"], Cell["magnetic = GaussianState[{\"SpinRotationZ\", \
\"Charge\"}, \"Filling\" -> 1/2, \"Magnetization\" -> \
m];\nToSpinChargeBasis[MeanField[U nOp[1, SpinUp] ** nOp[1, SpinDown], \
magnetic]]\n(* m^2 U - 2 m U Sz_1 + U n_1/2 - U/4 *)", "Input"], 
  Cell["Six fermions are no harder. The fields are the coefficients of the \
result: the hopping field that the chirality produces on the bond i -> j, \
with symbolic sites:", "Text"], Cell["fields = \
OperatorCoefficients[MeanField[-Jc SdotScrossS[i, j, k], \
SpinSingletState[]]];\nfields[cDag[i, SpinUp] ** c[j, SpinUp]]\n(* (3 I/4) Jc \
chi_jk Conjugate[chi_ik], that is (3 I/4) Jc chi_jk chi_ki *)", "Input"], 
  Cell["9. Wick's theorem meets the exact solution", "Section"], 
  Cell["The ground state of free fermions is Gaussian, so Wick's theorem must \
be exact there. A ring of six sites with three electrons of each spin: its \
contractions from the exact ground state, then the symbolic result of Wick's \
theorem with them, against the exact value.", "Text"], 
  Cell["freeSector = FockSector[FockSpace[Range[6]], \"N\" -> 6, \"Sz\" -> \
0];\nfree = First[Last[LowestStates[FockMatrix[hubbard[6] /. {t -> 1, U -> \
0}, freeSector], 1]]];\nchi12 = FockExpectation[cDag[1, SpinUp] ** c[2, \
SpinUp], free, freeSector]\n(* 0.333...: one third *)", "Input"], 
  Cell["{WickVEV[SdotS[1, 2], SpinSingletState[]] /. \[Chi][1, 2] -> chi12, \
FockExpectation[SdotS[1, 2], free, freeSector]}\n(* {-0.1667, -0.1667}: \
Wick's theorem, and the exact value *)", "Input"], 
  Cell["10. Gutzwiller projectors", "Section"], 
  Cell["FermiWick`Gutzwiller` handles projected operators: the hopping \
between two Gutzwiller-projected sites is reduced by a factor, here as a \
function of the variational angle theta.", "Text"], 
  Cell["real = GaussianState[{\"SpinRotation\", \"Charge\", \
\"TimeReversal\"}, \"Filling\" -> 1/2];\nhop = Sum[PlusHC[cDag[1, s] ** c[2, \
s]], {s, $Spins}];\nSimplify[GutzwillerVEV[LocalSandwich[GutzwillerProjector, \
{1, 2}, hop], real]/WickVEV[hop, real]]\n(* Sin[2 theta]^2: the Gutzwiller \
reduction of the hopping, 8 d (1 - 2 d) with d = Sin[theta]^2/2 *)", 
   "Input"], Cell["11. LaTeX", "Section"], Cell["ToTeX[ToSpinChargeBasis[Oper\
atorCommutator[SdotS[1, 2], SdotS[2, 3]]]]\n(* \"-i \\mathbf \
S_{1}\\cdot(\\mathbf S_{2}\\times\\mathbf S_{3})\" *)", "Input"], 
  Cell["WickTeX[cDag[1, SpinUp] ** cDag[2, SpinDown] ** c[3, SpinDown] ** \
c[4, SpinUp], SpinSingletState[\"Pairing\" -> \[CapitalDelta]]]\n(* Wick's \
theorem written out, with the contractions drawn (LaTeX package simpler-wick) \
*)", "Input"], Cell["Where next", "Section"], 
  Cell["The wiki (github.com/n4sd4q/FermiWick/wiki) has the recipes: writing \
operators, declaring a state, mean field, exact diagonalisation, Gutzwiller \
projectors, LaTeX. The showcase goes through the physics, and the manual \
describes every function; ?FermiWick`* lists the functions and ?Name explains \
each one.", "Text"]}, Visible -> True, 
 WindowTitle -> "FermiWick \[Dash] guided tour", 
 StyleDefinitions -> "Default.nb"]
